      <div style="flex: 3 1 560px; min-width: 0; border-top: 2px solid #121212;">

        <details class="faq" style="border-bottom: 1px solid #C9C7BF; padding: 24px 0;">
          <summary class="disp" style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 24px; font-weight: 700; font-size: 22px; line-height: 1.3;"><span>Should I start with the Foundation or go straight to the Seat?</span><span class="faq-ico" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
          <div style="margin-top: 16px; max-width: 720px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: #3A3A37; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px;">
            <p style="margin: 0;">If you already know you need someone to own this, go straight to the Seat. That is the common path for founders who have felt the cost of carrying the company alone for long enough.</p>
            <p style="margin: 0;">Start with the Foundation if you want the foundation built first, with a concrete plan in hand, before committing to a multi-month engagement. It is a real product on its own, not a trial.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq" style="border-bottom: 1px solid #C9C7BF; padding: 24px 0;">
          <summary class="disp" style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 24px; font-weight: 700; font-size: 22px; line-height: 1.3;"><span>Is this a strategy deck, or do you actually run it?</span><span class="faq-ico" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
          <div style="margin-top: 16px; max-width: 720px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: #3A3A37;">
            <p style="margin: 0;">I run it. The Seat is mostly execution: communications, the website, sales enablement, launches and demand, shaped by whatever the priorities call for. The foundation is what keeps all of it coherent.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq" style="border-bottom: 1px solid #C9C7BF; padding: 24px 0;">
          <summary class="disp" style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 24px; font-weight: 700; font-size: 22px; line-height: 1.3;"><span>We already have a junior marketer. Do we need this?</span><span class="faq-ico" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
          <div style="margin-top: 16px; max-width: 720px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: #3A3A37; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px;">
            <p style="margin: 0;">A junior marketer can execute well, but they should not be expected to own the positioning, GTM strategy and commercial planning for the business. That is where companies can end up with good tactics but no clear direction, while the founder is still carrying the bigger commercial decisions.</p>
            <p style="margin: 0;">The Seat provides that senior-level ownership and can work alongside an existing marketer, giving them clear priorities and a stronger foundation to execute against.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq" style="border-bottom: 1px solid #C9C7BF; padding: 24px 0;">
          <summary class="disp" style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 24px; font-weight: 700; font-size: 22px; line-height: 1.3;"><span>When does the Seat make sense instead of a permanent hire?</span><span class="faq-ico" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
          <div style="margin-top: 16px; max-width: 720px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: #3A3A37; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 14px;">
            <p style="margin: 0;">The Seat works when a company needs senior marketing ownership now, but is not yet ready to build the permanent function around it. It creates the positioning, priorities and in-market motion the business needs today, while making the longer-term marketing structure much clearer.</p>
            <p style="margin: 0;">For some companies, that becomes an ongoing fractional model. For others, it creates a stronger foundation for the permanent team or leader that comes next.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq" style="border-bottom: 1px solid #C9C7BF; padding: 24px 0;">
          <summary class="disp" style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 24px; font-weight: 700; font-size: 22px; line-height: 1.3;"><span>What does this cost, and what am I committing to?</span><span class="faq-ico" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
          <div style="margin-top: 16px; max-width: 720px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: #3A3A37;">
            <p style="margin: 0;">The Foundation is a fixed fee for four weeks, ending in a clear foundation and plan. The Seat runs three to six months, six is ideal, with the monthly fee scoped in conversation based on priorities. Either way, you know what you are getting and for how long before anything starts.</p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="faq" style="border-bottom: 1px solid #C9C7BF; padding: 24px 0;">
          <summary class="disp" style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; gap: 24px; font-weight: 700; font-size: 22px; line-height: 1.3;"><span>What happens on the first conversation?</span><span class="faq-ico" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
          <div style="margin-top: 16px; max-width: 720px; font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.65; color: #3A3A37;">
            <p style="margin: 0;">It is a short conversation to check fit, not a pitch. We talk about where the company is, where growth is stalling, and whether this is the right next step. If it is not a fit, you will hear that directly.</p>
